1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,704
One server runs a database and needs speed. Another is a laptop and needs battery.

2
00:00:05,724 --> 00:00:09,340
Linux can change its whole tuning with a single command.

3
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:12,222
In this video: manage tuning profiles.

4
00:00:16,742 --> 00:00:19,325
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:21,311
Manage tuning profiles.

6
00:00:21,781 --> 00:00:26,004
We'll install the tuning service, look at the profiles, switch to another one,

7
00:00:26,024 --> 00:00:28,289
and see that the choice survives a reboot.

8
00:00:37,809 --> 00:00:42,910
tuned is a service that applies a bundle of system settings, called a profile.

9
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:45,405
The command to manage it is tuned-adm.

10
00:00:53,875 --> 00:00:58,979
Each profile is made for a job. virtual-guest is for virtual machines.

11
00:00:58,999 --> 00:01:03,663
throughput-performance is for raw speed. powersave saves energy.

12
00:01:03,683 --> 00:01:06,079
balanced is a general middle way.

13
00:01:18,549 --> 00:01:24,204
The command tuned-adm recommend suggests the best profile for the machine it runs on.

14
00:01:31,724 --> 00:01:37,741
First: is tuned installed? rpm -q asks the package database. Not installed here.

15
00:01:43,211 --> 00:01:48,084
So we install it with dnf. The option -y answers yes to the questions.

16
00:01:48,104 --> 00:01:52,068
It adds the tuned package and sixty-nine helper packages,

17
00:01:52,088 --> 00:01:57,101
which we skip in the video. The install even enables the service for us.

18
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:20,087
systemctl enable --now makes a service start at every boot, and starts it right now.

19
00:02:20,107 --> 00:02:24,137
It prints nothing, because the install had already enabled it.

20
00:02:35,607 --> 00:02:38,702
systemctl is-active confirms: active.

21
00:02:44,222 --> 00:02:49,489
Now we look. tuned-adm active shows the profile in use: virtual-guest.

22
00:02:49,509 --> 00:02:52,963
And tuned-adm recommend suggests the same one,

23
00:02:52,983 --> 00:02:56,362
so our machine already has the right profile.

24
00:03:09,882 --> 00:03:14,013
tuned-adm list shows every profile with a short description.

25
00:03:14,033 --> 00:03:19,547
The pipe and head -n 12 keep the first twelve lines. Here are the first of them.

26
00:03:27,067 --> 00:03:32,207
To switch, use tuned-adm profile and the name. We pick throughput-performance.

27
00:03:32,227 --> 00:03:35,580
It needs sudo, and it prints nothing when it works.

28
00:03:46,050 --> 00:03:49,444
tuned-adm active confirms the new profile.

29
00:03:54,964 --> 00:03:58,400
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

30
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:06,062
We reboot. Afterwards, tuned-adm active still says throughput-performance.

31
00:04:06,082 --> 00:04:10,891
The choice is saved in the file /etc/tuned/active_profile,

32
00:04:10,911 --> 00:04:13,639
and the service reads it at boot.

33
00:04:24,109 --> 00:04:28,057
To finish, we go back to virtual-guest, the recommended profile.

34
00:04:34,577 --> 00:04:38,743
Your turn. Install tuned and start it. Set the profile to powersave.

35
00:04:38,763 --> 00:04:40,466
Reboot, and run the checker.

36
00:04:46,936 --> 00:04:48,687
Try before you open the solution.

37
00:04:54,207 --> 00:04:57,920
Next up: locate and interpret system log files and journals.

38
00:04:58,390 --> 00:05:01,293
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
